How JSX Really Compiles
-----
	Undre the hood, JSX is transformed into calls to React.createElement

	return (
	<div className="App">
		<h1> 

		Let's get stareted </h1>
	</div>
	)


	what it becomes (conceptually):

		return React.createElement('div', {className:'App'}, React.createElement('h1', {}, "Let's get started"))


- Use fragment <> ..</> -> groups elements without adding a real, visible wrapper element to the actual DOM. 

return(
	
	<>
		<h2>
		</p>
	</>

)


State: data that may change over time. When it changes, the UI should update to reflect it. Each component can maintain its OWN state, and multiple compoments can share state.

Event: event handlers are added via PROPS to built-in components (like <button>). You pass a function as the value - react calls it when that event happens.

Why - title not changed? - mechanical explaination:
-----
	- A component function only RUNS (and returns fresh JSX) when react calls it - normally once, when the component first placed on the page (mounted, via parent rendering it)
	- clicking the button runs clickHandler - but clickHandler resasisning a local variable doesn't, by itself, tell React 'Please re-run Expenseitem and re-render its JSX'

	you will need an explicit way to tell React ' Something changed, please re-evaluate this component'. That mechanism is STATE.


useState - a function provided by React that lets a varaible be traced as STATE. 
			changing a piece of state (via its setter function) teslls react to re-evaluate (re-run) that component funciton only.


e.preventDefault() --> stops the browser's default full page reload/navigation behavior. 
						This is the standard pattern for EVERY form submission in React SPA 


Two way binding:
-----

- data currently flows ONE way: input -> state (via onChange). The input's visible content is still just the browser's own DOM value - React isn't actually controlling what is displayed, only reading it.










