What?
	React - JavaScript Library for building user interfaces.


Why not just JavaScript?
	- Manually using document.getElementByID(...).innerHTML ==> across 100+ elements quickly becomes combersome, error-prone, and hard to maintain
	- React offers simpler mental model: 
		Describe - WHAT UI Should look like for given state,
		let React figure out the actual DOM instructions


	Imperative (Vanilla JS) - you give step-by-step instructiions: 'find this element, then change its text, then add this class..'
	Declarative (React)		- you describe the DESIRED END STATE , react works out the DOM changes needed to get there.



CRA - Create React App - classic/original tool, npx create-react-app my-app
Vite -  Modern tool: npm create vite@latest, 
		updated on save near-instantly (no full reload),
		produces smaller/faster production builds,
		uses browser's native ES moduels during development


WhAT IS JSX?
	- JSX = JavaScript XML - writing HTML like markup directly inside JavaScript
	- Not valid in browser as -is - bul tool -> transform it into plain JavaScript before your code ever runs!


	function App() {

		return(
			<div>
				<h1>Let's get started. </h1>
			</div>

		)

	}



Component:
	- speifically; a funciton that reutnrs JSX
	- Naming convention: PasalCase, and finenae usually matches the component name
	- One file, one component, is the standard convention 



ONE ROOT ELEMENT RULE:
	A component's JSX must return exactly ONE root element. 
	<div className="APP"> ..</div>
	is that single root here. everything. else nests inside it. 

- Each component has its OWN CSS file, imported directly into the component file - this keeps styling co-loacated with the component that uses it.
- className - connects the JSX element to a CSS class exactly like in plain HTML/CSS.


Props:
	component cannot reach into another component's variable directly - each comopnent fucntion is its own isolated scope
	"props" - PARENT can hands data DOWN to a CHILD - 


App (Holds data)
	-> Expenses (renders list)
		-> ExpenseItem (renders one card)
			-> ExpenseDate (renders the Date badge)



PRoblem:
	duplicate CSS
		- ExpenseItem.css and Expenses.css both declare border-radius and box-shadow - exact same visual 'card' treatement
		- solution - extract reusable card wrapper component that any component can wrap itself it in to get that shared look



	props.children - key concept
		- whateer JSX you put between a component opening and closing tags is automatically avaialble inside that ocmponent as props.children

		


